WEBVTT

NOTE Sick Leave and Serious Illness — CFHR training, current as of 10.31.2026. Built from the narration manifest by scripts/build-vtt.mjs.

1
00:00:07.235 --> 00:00:09.998
This is manager training on California sick leave

2
00:00:09.998 --> 00:00:15.037
and serious illness, current as of October 31st, 2026.

3
00:00:15.037 --> 00:00:17.617
It's a framework, not legal advice.

4
00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:20.137
Your employee calls in sick on a Monday.

5
00:00:20.137 --> 00:00:21.437
It might be two days.

6
00:00:21.437 --> 00:00:22.982
It might be four months.

7
00:00:22.982 --> 00:00:25.786
Either way, two questions decide everything:

8
00:00:25.786 --> 00:00:27.370
is his job protected,

9
00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.588
and is anything replacing his pay?

10
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.630
Watch the answers change as the illness does.

11
00:00:34.894 --> 00:00:37.454
Stage one is ordinary sick days, and paid sick

12
00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:39.770
leave is the one leave that does both.

13
00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.477
It protects the job, and the employer

14
00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:43.224
pays it at the regular rate.

15
00:00:43.224 --> 00:00:45.122
Every employer owes it.

16
00:00:45.175 --> 00:00:46.921
He earns at least an hour for every

17
00:00:46.921 --> 00:00:49.603
thirty worked, usable from his ninetieth day

18
00:00:49.603 --> 00:00:51.920
on the job, and the employer may cap use

19
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.154
at forty hours, or five days, a year.

20
00:00:55.506 --> 00:00:57.252
He tells you as soon as practical;

21
00:00:57.252 --> 00:00:58.837
out loud is enough.

22
00:00:58.837 --> 00:01:01.275
Don't make him find a replacement, and don't deny

23
00:01:01.275 --> 00:01:03.917
the day just because there's no doctor's note.

24
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:06.126
Pay it by the next regular payday.

25
00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:10.338
Thursday, his doctor says

26
00:01:10.338 --> 00:01:11.720
it's more than a few days:

27
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.645
a serious health condition, and weeks off work.

28
00:01:14.645 --> 00:01:15.580
Now the job

29
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:18.040
and the pay split into two systems.

30
00:01:18.108 --> 00:01:20.912
On the job side, CFRA holds his job

31
00:01:20.912 --> 00:01:23.228
for up to twelve weeks, at five or more

32
00:01:23.228 --> 00:01:25.706
employees, if he has twelve months of service

33
00:01:25.706 --> 00:01:28.557
and twelve hundred fifty hours in the past year.

34
00:01:28.557 --> 00:01:31.605
At fifty or more employees within seventy-five miles,

35
00:01:31.605 --> 00:01:34.977
federal FMLA runs at the same time.

36
00:01:34.977 --> 00:01:37.131
Together, not one after the other:

37
00:01:37.131 --> 00:01:39.484
twelve weeks, not twenty-four.

38
00:01:42.425 --> 00:01:44.823
The pay moves to State Disability Insurance,

39
00:01:44.823 --> 00:01:47.576
paid by EDD, not the employer.

40
00:01:47.635 --> 00:01:49.301
It replaces seventy to ninety percent

41
00:01:49.301 --> 00:01:52.836
of his wages, up to $1,765 a week,

42
00:01:52.836 --> 00:01:55.437
but only after a seven-day unpaid wait.

43
00:01:55.437 --> 00:01:56.686
See that gap?

44
00:01:56.752 --> 00:02:00.124
Remaining sick leave or PTO can bridge it.

45
00:02:00.124 --> 00:02:02.400
Then SDI can keep paying as long

46
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.538
as his doctor certifies

47
00:02:03.538 --> 00:02:06.289
the disability, up to fifty-two weeks.

48
00:02:08.765 --> 00:02:10.633
The paperwork splits the same way.

49
00:02:10.633 --> 00:02:12.788
Leave paperwork goes to the employer.

50
00:02:12.788 --> 00:02:14.210
He asks for the time off as soon

51
00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:17.605
as practicable, and he doesn't have to name CFRA.

52
00:02:17.673 --> 00:02:21.411
The employer responds within five business days, designates

53
00:02:21.411 --> 00:02:26.002
the leave as CFRA, and tells him.

54
00:02:26.002 --> 00:02:28.806
If it requires a medical certification, it allows

55
00:02:28.806 --> 00:02:31.469
at least fifteen calendar days to return it.

56
00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:34.580
Pay paperwork goes to EDD.

57
00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:38.278
He files his claim, the DE 2501, no later

58
00:02:38.278 --> 00:02:41.310
than forty-nine days after the disability begins.

59
00:02:41.378 --> 00:02:46.823
The employer returns EDD's notice, the DE 2503,

60
00:02:46.823 --> 00:02:49.290
within two working days of receiving it.

61
00:02:53.185 --> 00:02:54.729
Now look at week twelve.

62
00:02:54.729 --> 00:02:58.101
CFRA and FMLA end here.

63
00:02:58.101 --> 00:02:59.361
SDI doesn't;

64
00:02:59.361 --> 00:03:01.392
it can keep paying long after.

65
00:03:01.392 --> 00:03:03.815
Paid is not the same as protected.

66
00:03:04.169 --> 00:03:06.403
If he's back, he returns to the same

67
00:03:06.403 --> 00:03:09.329
or a comparable job, and a return-to-work release can

68
00:03:09.329 --> 00:03:12.766
be required only under a uniformly applied policy.

69
00:03:12.815 --> 00:03:14.522
If he's still out, week twelve

70
00:03:14.522 --> 00:03:16.669
is a checkpoint, not an end.

71
00:03:16.734 --> 00:03:20.797
Before any end-of-leave decision, run the interactive process:

72
00:03:20.797 --> 00:03:23.398
under California's disability law, more leave

73
00:03:23.398 --> 00:03:26.591
for a finite period may be a reasonable accommodation.

74
00:03:30.664 --> 00:03:31.883
So the bottom line.

75
00:03:31.883 --> 00:03:33.427
Sick leave does both.

76
00:03:33.427 --> 00:03:35.215
A serious illness splits them:

77
00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:38.141
CFRA holds the job for up to twelve weeks;

78
00:03:38.141 --> 00:03:40.728
SDI can pay for up to fifty-two.

79
00:03:40.788 --> 00:03:43.429
Paid is not the same as protected, so treat

80
00:03:43.429 --> 00:03:46.267
week twelve as a checkpoint, not an end.
